UPSCALE METHOD AND ACTUAL RESULTS
The separately delivered 6144x4096 PNG is genuine 4x inference using the official Real-ESRGAN x4plus anime 6B model through NCNN CPU. The 3072x2048 PNG is a Pillow Lanczos reduction of that 4x result, not a native 2x model result. AI upscaling does not restore unavailable ground-truth detail.
A continuous original is processed in 70 computational tiles using 160px cores, 32px context, and 16px additional reflected input padding. Context is discarded without blending; the output canvas is assembled from those inferred core pixels. This is computational tiling, not artistic scene stitching.
This scene's completed inference wall time: 197.691 seconds; peak child RSS: 3316976 KiB. Two full scenes were run concurrently during recovery. Scripts request 2 threads after model loading; the load-time NCNN setting is 4 threads. OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 is also set.
Two boundary-junction checks compare the actual full output pixels against independent untiled inference in metrics/seam_metrics.json. They assess computational boundary consistency, not ground-truth reconstruction quality or all possible boundaries.

HERO SCALE AND REVIEW METHOD
The unchanged RGBA hero reference is alpha-cropped at alpha > 30, resized with Lanczos to 160px visible height, and composited conventionally with a small shadow. The adopted art scale is a 64px hero equivalent in the native source, or 256px at raw 4x scale. Raw AI4x is reduced by 0.625 to 3840x2560, equivalent to a 2.5x scene display scale. Each 1536x1024 review viewport therefore spans 614.4x409.6 native pixels. Exact crop boxes, foot locations and scale conversions are in review_config.json and review_views_metadata.json.
Three meaningful views were visually inspected for an unclipped hero standing on apparently clear ground. Hero and shadow overlays appear only in review previews, never in the native or upscaled fullscenes. These are scale illustrations, not gameplay validation. Baked trees, walls, stairs, pillars and props need explicit collision and foreground occlusion work. Numerical camera angles, world coverage and engine scale were not verified.

